<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      15-01. The permission that gates reading the tenant's audit log.

      audit-service had no user-facing endpoint and no gateway route, so the audit trail was
      unreachable from outside the cluster: `grep -c audit gateway/.../application.yml` returned 0.
      GET /api/v1/audit/events is that route's destination and this is its gate.

      ── Why a new code rather than reusing rbac.manage or a reporting permission ──

      The audit log is not implied by any capability that already exists. It shows every login,
      every failed login, every void and refund, every role grant and every password reset in the
      tenant — including those of the person reading it. That makes it both the surface a
      compromised administrator account would most want and the record that would expose the
      compromise, which is an argument for gating it on its own code that can be granted and
      revoked without dragging an unrelated capability along.

      ── Who holds it ──

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN. Not MANAGER, and that exclusion is the point: MANAGER holds
      pos.order.void.any and finance.expense.approve, so a manager is a subject of this log. A
      manager who could also read it could see whether anyone had looked, which is the one property
      an audit trail must not have. ACCOUNTANT is likewise excluded — a financial reviewer needs
      the ledger, not the security log; if that turns out to be wrong it is one grant to add, and
      adding a grant later is recoverable in a way that un-disclosing a log is not.

      Granted EXPLICITLY to both roles rather than relying on 057's catalogue-wide repair. Every
      blanket grant in this changelog (030/041/042/045/046/057) is point-in-time: it enumerates
      what existed on the day it ran and retro-grants nothing added afterwards. 055 documents that
      property and grants explicitly for the same reason. A permission nobody holds produces a
      clean, confident 403 for every user including OWNER, which is the exact defect class
      PermissionCatalogClosureTest exists to catch.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-059-audit-log-view" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('audit.log.view', 'audit', 'Read the tenant''s audit trail (logins, voids, refunds, role changes, password resets)')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'audit.log.view'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'audit.log.view')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'audit.log.view';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'audit.log.view';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
